```java
String displayConstraintListC = progressCalback.displayConstraintList(C);
String displayConstraintListAC = progressCalback.displayConstraintList(AC);
```
Reason:
The lack of null checks for the `progressCalback` object before invoking its methods means that if `progressCalback` is not initialized (i.e., it is null), the program will attempt to call methods on a null reference, resulting in `NullPointerExceptions`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
String displayConstraintListC = progressCalback == null ? null : progressCalback.displayConstraintList(C);
String displayConstraintListAC = progressCalback == null ? null : progressCalback.displayConstraintList(AC);
```
Reason:
These statements introduce null checks for the `progressCalback` object before invoking its methods. If `progressCalback` is null, the method calls will not be executed, thus preventing `NullPointerExceptions` from occurring.


